---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../components/Showcase.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../components/StylingHooks.astro";

const import_ = `@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/scrollbar.css";`;

const always = `<div
  class="sk-scrollbar demo-scroll"
  tabindex="0"
>
  <p>
    Region with native scroll and system chrome: track and thumb
    always visible. The wheel, the momentum and the touch continue
    being from the platform; the pattern only paints the surface
    that the browser exposes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Add <code>sk-scrollbar</code> to any container with
    <code>overflow: auto</code> (or <code>scroll</code>). Without the
    class, the OS paints its native scrollbar.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third paragraph to force overflow and see the
    thumb. Change mode or contrast above: hooks continue
    to semantic tokens.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fourth paragraph. The size of the thumb is decided by the browser
    depending on how much content there is; the system only sets the width
    of the rail and the colors.
  </p>
</div>`;

const reveal = `<div
  class="sk-scrollbar sk-scrollbar--reveal demo-scroll"
  tabindex="0"
>
  <p>
    Same region, variant <code>sk-scrollbar--reveal</code>:
    the gutter is reserved (<code>scrollbar-gutter: stable</code>)
    but the thumb is transparent until you hover or
    keyboard focus enters the region.
  </p>
  <p>
    When revealing, a filled track does not appear: only the thumb,
    where are you A painted rail would read as an edge that the
    layout did not ask for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mouse over here, or tab to this box, to
    see the thumb. When he leaves, he remains silent again without moving his
    layout.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ideal on sidebars and dense rails, where a scrollbar
    always on competes with the content.
  </p>
</div>`;
---

<Base
  title="Scrollbar"
  description="Pattern opt-in que pinta la scrollbar nativa del navegador: siempre visible, o silenciosa hasta hover/foco."
>
  <h1>Scrollbar</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    It does not replace the native scroll. Color the surface that the browser already exposes
    (<code>scrollbar-color</code>, <code>scrollbar-width</code> and the pseudo-elements
    <code>::-webkit-scrollbar*</code>). The wheel, the momentum, the touch pad and the
    OS preferences remain platform-specific.
  </p>

  <h2>Why is it a pattern?</h2>
  <p>
    Several hosts share the same rail: the docs sidebar, the drawer, the
    customize. Sending the paint once prevents each surface from reinventing a thumb.
    It is <strong>opt-in</strong>, like nav-list: an app can keep the OS scrollbar.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />
  <p>
    Then mark the region that is already scrolling (<code>overflow: auto</code> or
    <code>scroll</code>, with a limited size). The pattern does not define the overflow: it only paints
    the scrollbar that the browser exposes.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`<!-- your container with overflow: auto -->
<div class="sk-scrollbar">…</div>

<!-- o la variante silenciosa hasta hover/foco -->
<div class="sk-scrollbar sk-scrollbar--reveal">…</div>`}
    label="HTML"
  />

  <h2>Siempre visible</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sk-scrollbar</code> leaves track and thumb visible. Use it when scrolling is part
    of the UI map (a code panel, a wide table).
  </p>
  <Showcase html={always} label="sk-scrollbar" />

  <h2>Reveal on hover (and focus)</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sk-scrollbar sk-scrollbar--reveal</code> reserves the gutter so as not to move the layout, and
    leave the thumb transparent until <code>:hover</code> or <code>:focus-within</code>. At
    reveal only the thumb appears, not a filled track.
  </p>
  <Showcase html={reveal} label="sk-scrollbar--reveal" note="hover or tab to reveal" />
  <p>
    The sidebar rail in this documentation uses exactly this variant: still at rest,
    legible when you touch it.
  </p>

  <h2>Which one to choose</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Always visible</strong> when the reader needs to know up front that there is more
      content (long lists, logs, editors).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reveal</strong> when chrome competes with content (navigation, touch,
      narrow panels). The stable gutter prevents CLS when the thumb appears.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Freezing the scroll of the <em>document</em> behind a modal is another pattern:
    <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="/en/scroll-lock">scroll lock</a>. They do not step on one another: one paints
    regions, the other freezes <code>html</code>.
  </p>

  <StylingHooks
    groups={[{ file: "patterns/scrollbar.css", probe: "sk-scrollbar" }]}
  />

  <h2>Contrato</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Opt-in: import <code>patterns/scrollbar.css</code> and mark the region with
      <code>sk-scrollbar</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The region must be scrollable (<code>overflow: auto|scroll</code> and a size
      bounded). The pattern does not define the overflow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-scrollbar--reveal</code> also requires <code>sk-scrollbar</code> (the variant
      only re-declare hooks; the base provides the rest).
    </li>
    <li>
      It does not invent a thumb in JS or hide the native scroll with a fake div. If the OS uses
      scrollbar overlay, the reveal gutter is a no-op and does not bother.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <code>--sk-scrollbar-*</code> hooks are stepped on from your CSS on
      <code>.sk-scrollbar</code>, just like any other styling hook.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Demo rails: enough height to overflow, so the thumb is real and not a theoretical claim. */
  :global(.demo-scroll) {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    block-size: 11rem;
    overflow: auto;
    padding: var(--space-inset-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-surface);
    background: var(--color-bg-surface);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  :global(.demo-scroll) p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  :global(.demo-scroll) p + p {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-stack-sm);
  }

  :global(.demo-scroll) code {
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }
</style>
